2186101337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Point that Lies on a Vertically Scaled Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100987 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cb1c0a6-992e-5f39-be35-0a80084ed650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Stretches of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35b6b482-18e8-514d-960d-4d6d3def3f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101361 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(8, 12)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. Which of the following points must lie on $y = 2f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03fd89d5-cc6f-56c7-a854-1b970edc5ff5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1061 457106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4c40517-5865-5128-af8f-564d6a22648d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1061 457107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60cd62a7-4991-57ac-90fb-b574ced67839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1061 457110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea2e9c61-fcab-5300-8818-9590ecbb72e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1061 457113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c481045b-1f32-5a1d-8b62-25e89a1b9393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1064 "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x),$ and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math]} \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0,2) & (0,4) &  \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value. \n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2.$ \n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1065 3284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bea7d899-701e-53ab-9bb8-42e452f0f5c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
